From: damnbitimcrit <333091414+damnbitimcrit@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [GPU] Let title code snapshot GPU-written memory without
 waiting

The counterpart of the guest readback that never waits for the host GPU,
for data a title wants to keep but reads only later. A guest thread posts
physical ranges into one of 16 slots (guest_readback.h): the parts whose
host GPU copy is newer than guest memory are listed under the global
lock, and the post returns at once. The command processor thread takes
posts lock-free right before executing each batch of the ring (and at
its loop top, idle wake-ups and in WAIT_REG_MEM's poll), so a copy is
recorded before any command the guest wrote after its post. At the take
it lists the parts again: if one listed at the post is gone (a load
replaced the data since), the job fails; otherwise it copies the current
parts, one consistent state, into its own 1 MiB readback buffer and ends
the submission unless that would wait for pipeline creation. Completed
copies are read into a host store at the usual completion pumps and
delivered with try_lock, so the thread never waits for a guest thread.

Guest threads can await a slot (the command processor thread then ends
and waits for the submission only if the copy isn't done yet), flush
before replacing the posted memory (waits until every post's copy is
recorded, or withdraws it), and drop slots. Nothing is written to guest
memory; the title takes the bytes and writes them where and when it
chooses. D3D12 and Vulkan.
